WEBVTT
TITLE Euler Trails and Hamiltonian Cycles: The One-Line Test and the Question That Has None — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
An Euler circuit uses every edge exactly once and returns to where it started.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.000
A Hamiltonian cycle visits every vertex exactly once and returns to the start.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Trail, path, circuit: a trail repeats no edge, a path repeats no vertex, a circuit is a closed trail.

4
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
The two questions are independent: the page shows a graph with each property and not the other.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Every time a trail passes through a vertex it uses two of that vertex's edges: one to arrive, one to leave.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:45.000
So a closed tour leaves every vertex with an even degree: the parity condition is necessary.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:53.000
An open trail has exactly two exceptions: the vertex it starts at and the vertex it ends at.

8
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Königsberg: the four land masses have degrees 3, 3, 3 and 5. Four odd vertices, so no trail at all.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
The converse is a theorem: connected with every degree even is necessary and sufficient.

10
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:15.000
The hypothesis is about the component that holds the edges: two disjoint triangles have all even degrees and no tour.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Join the triangles at one vertex and the tour exists: the shared vertex is used twice, once per triangle.

12
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Directed version: in-degree equals out-degree everywhere, and the vertices with edges are in one component.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Hierholzer's idea: walk from any vertex until you are stuck, then splice in what is left.

14
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.000
From vertex 0 the greedy walk closes immediately: 0, 1, 2, 0. Three of the six edges are used.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Vertex 1 still has unused edges, so splice its cycle 1, 3, 4, 1 into the tour at vertex 1.

16
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Every edge is taken once and each splice is a pointer change, so the decision and the tour are linear.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.000
An Eulerian graph splits into edge-disjoint cycles, and that splitting is the proof of sufficiency.

18
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.000
K5 splits into two 5-cycles that share all five vertices and no edge: 0,1,2,3,4,0 and 0,2,4,1,3,0.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:23.000
K5 has 132 Euler circuits when a circuit is a cyclic edge sequence, counted once up to rotation and reversal.

20
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:30.000
The count is not what GATE asks for. What it asks is whether a tour exists, and the criterion answers that.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Deciding whether a graph has a Hamiltonian cycle is NP-complete.

22
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Brute force on the complete graph would examine (n − 1)! / 2 cycles: 181,440 at n = 10 and about 3.1 × 10^23 at n = 25.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:53.000
So a sufficient condition is a certificate, never a test: it can say yes, and it can never say no.

24
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
One question has a linear test, the other has none known. That asymmetry is the point of the pair.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
Dirac, 1952: with at least three vertices, if every degree is at least n/2 then a Hamiltonian cycle exists.

26
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Ore, 1960: if every pair of non-adjacent vertices has degree sum at least n, a Hamiltonian cycle exists.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:23.000
The six-cycle fails Dirac and fails Ore, and is Hamiltonian: both theorems are sufficient, not necessary.

28
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Necessary conditions that do work: a bipartite graph needs equal sides, and c(G − S) is at most |S|.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.000
The Petersen graph: ten vertices, fifteen edges, every degree three, girth five.

30
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:45.000
It has no Hamiltonian cycle, and the page proves it by exhausting every cycle, not by inspecting the picture.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:53.000
Delete any single vertex and the rest is Hamiltonian. That property is called hypohamiltonian.

32
00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:00.000
It also has no Euler trail: all ten degrees are odd, so the two questions both answer no, for different reasons.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
GATE CSE 2007, Question 23: which of these graphs has an Eulerian circuit?

34
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Option A fails: a 2-regular graph can be two disjoint triangles, all even and not Eulerian.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Option B fails: every vertex of K(90) has degree 89, which is odd. Option C: the complement has degree 22 everywhere.

36
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
Option C is the answer: every degree 22, one component, so an Euler circuit exists.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.000
First question: does the problem ration edges or vertices? That decides which criterion applies.

38
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Edge question: count degrees, count odd vertices, then check that the edges sit in one component.

39
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:52.000
Vertex question: try Dirac and Ore to certify a cycle, and look for a reason it cannot exist.

40
00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Five traps, one defence: name the object, name the hypothesis, and never let one answer the other question.

